    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That craftsman analogy works well for the slide. Could you expand a bit on the 'fault tolerance' part? I want to make sure the VP understands the risk of vertical scaling if a single instance fails, vs the resilience we get with horizontal scaling. Maybe add a quick point about what happens if one of those 'craftsmen' goes offline.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fault tolerance is a critical aspect of horizontal scaling. When you have multiple servers handling different parts of the workload, if one server fails or goes offline, the others can pick up the slack, ensuring that the application remains responsive. This is because each server is working on a specific task, and if one server fails, the others can compensate for the loss.\n\nIn contrast, vertical scaling can be a single point of failure. If a single server fails, the entire application can come crashing down, as all the tasks are being handled by that one server. This is why horizontal scaling is often preferred in high-traffic or high-availability applications.\n\nTo illustrate this, think of the craftsmen again. If one of them goes offline, the others can continue working on their tasks, and the project can still be completed. But if the entire workshop is built around one skilled craftsman, and he goes offline, the entire project comes to a grinding halt. In our case, we want to ensure that our application is like the workshop with multiple skilled craftsmen, where if one goes offline, the others can pick up the slack and keep the application running smoothly.",
      "source": "target",
